<methodology>
Enumerate state-changing endpoints and audit their anti-CSRF controls.
1. Sinks. Every non-idempotent handler: POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE routes, GraphQL
   mutations, RPC/action endpoints, one-GET-click state changes (GET that
   mutates), WebSocket messages that mutate state.
2. Token presence + enforcement. Find the CSRF token mechanism (framework
   middleware, decorator, form field, header such as X-CSRF-Token). The check
   counts only if it is REQUIRED on the handler: a check that runs only when a
   token is supplied, a token compared non-constant-time, or a token not bound
   to the session/user is a bypass, not a control.
3. Origin/Referer validation. Missing, substring-matched (attacker.com.evil.io),
   or skipped-on-missing-header checks are gaps.
4. Cookie scoping. Session/auth cookie without SameSite=Lax/Strict (or
   explicitly SameSite=None without a justified cross-site use), plus no
   secondary token, means cookie-based auth alone will ride a cross-site
   request.
5. Content-Type reliance. A handler trusting "browsers won't send this
   cross-site" (JSON/text-plain) without a token is bypassable via form-encoded
   or multipart submissions when the parser tolerates them.
6. Login/state-changing GET. Mutating GET endpoints are CSRF-able by URL alone.

A path is VULNERABLE if a state-changing handler relies solely on cookie auth
(or no auth) with no effective token + Origin/SameSite control.
</methodology>

<examples>
Illustrative vulnerable patterns (different languages; NOT specific to this repo —
do not assume these files exist here; use them to recognise the shape of the bug):
  - python: `@app.post("/profile")` handler updating the DB with no CSRF
    dependency/middleware and a session cookie with SameSite not set.
    why: state change rides the victim's cookie cross-site.
  - java: `if (request.getParameter("csrf") != null) { verifyToken(...); }`
    why: token check only runs when the attacker-supplied form includes the
    field — omitting it skips validation entirely.
  - express: `app.post("/transfer", cookieAuth)` with no csurf/origin check and
    `cookie.parse` accepting `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` bodies.
</examples>

<false_positives_to_avoid>
- Endpoints already wrapped by verified CSRF middleware (framework-level
  enforcement you have read — cite it) are NOT findings.
- Bearer-token-in-header auth (token not auto-attached by the browser) is not
  classically CSRF-able — the attacker cannot force the header cross-site.
- Idempotent, non-state-changing reads (GET that only renders) are not CSRF
  findings even when tokenless.
- JSON-only endpoints that hard-require `application/json` with a strict parser
  are hard to trigger cross-site — note as low/informational, not a finding,
  unless you found a tolerant parser in code.
</false_positives_to_avoid>

Confidence: High = state-changing handler with no token/origin/SameSite control
anywhere on its path (read end to end). Medium = a control exists but is
provably skippable/unbound. Low = suspicious gap with unread framework glue.
Rule: when uncertain, round down to minimise false positives.

Begin by reading the entry-point files above (or, if none, list_dir the scope),
then audit every state-changing handler for its anti-CSRF control.

